A||N||6||johnmac||EGR Tube Gasket Location||||12||
Z||000000||johnmac||06-12-2001||04:16 PM||||I am installing a new EGR Tube on my 86GT, 2.8 V6 engine.<P>I have two gaskets, one with a smaller aperture than the other.<P>Which one goes where?<P>Does the smaller hole gasket go on the 'Hot Manifold'?<P>HELP!!!<P>Cordially,<P>John<P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||12
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||04:46 PM||||Im about 90% sure the thick one goes up by the intake plenium, and the thin one with the small hole goes down on the hot end.<P>  for some reason my gasket set had two of the thick ones, and one looked like graphite, so I used them instead of the one with the small hole.<P>  check the distance between the stud holes - they may be different sizes, which might answer your question.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||06-12-2001||04:56 PM||||This is what the installation instruction that come with a new GM pipe say:<P>1. Disconnect negative battery cable from battery.<BR>2. Remove distributor cap and cover lower housing with clean rag.<BR>3. Remove failed EGR valve adapter pipe assembly & discard.<BR>4. Remove old gaskets and discard<BR>5. Clean gasket surfaces on inlet plenum and EGR valve adaper<BR>6. install new EGR valve adapter pipe assembly & gasket.<BR>   IMPORTANT:<BR>   GASKET WITH STEEL INSERT HAVING SMALL CENTER HOLE MUST BE INSTALLED AT THE INLET PLENUM END OF THE EGR VALVE ADAPTER PIPE ASSEMBLY, WITH SIDE IDENTIFIED AS "TUBE SIDE" TOWARDS PIPE ASSEMBLY.<BR>7. Torque EGR valve adapter tube fasteners to  8-12 Nm.<BR>8. Connect distributor cap to lower housing.<BR>9. Connect negative battery cable.<P>------------------<BR> 85 Fiero GT<BR> 3.2 Liter Under Construction||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||05:03 PM||||okay I guess mine are wrong then - the gaskets that came in the set didnt look like any of the ones that were on the car when i took the egr pipe off - must be a redesign on the gaskets.<P>  now where did I put that one with the little hole?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||06-12-2001||05:07 PM||||Between the pipe and the plenum. So big hole at EGR, and little hole at Plenum. :)||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||06-12-2001||05:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>Between the pipe and the plenum. So big hole at EGR, and little hole at Plenum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>In case it matters at this point, I concur.  That's the way I found the gaskets on my 1986 SE V6 (purchased new), and that's the way the instuctions with my replacement EGR pipe said to put it together.<P>On that car, I managed to replace the pipe without removing the upper plenum, but it isn't the easiest thing to thread those bolts from underneath without cross-threading them in the aluminum.  Took patience and many cycles of "take a deep breath and start again."<P>Nowadays, if I needed to replace the EGR pipe, I would probably consider taking loose the plenum, for better access.<P>I have often wondered why they didn't use studs on the plenum end, and put bolts on the EGR valve end?  Seems like it would be easier to R&R the EGR pipe that way.   Maybe I'll give it a try when I put my GT back together, and see if it works out.||||reg||3
Z||000006||johnmac||06-12-2001||07:01 PM||||GOT IT!<P>FANTASTIC!<P>You Fiero Nuts are the best. Glad to be a member of this 'nutty' society.<P>Thanks Again.<P>John||||reg||1
